.TH FLUXLAND-APPS 5 "2026-10-25" "fluxland 2.0.0" "File Formats Manual"
.SH NAME
fluxland-apps \- per-window settings (the Fluxbox apps file)
.SH DESCRIPTION
The apps file says where windows open and how they look: their
workspace, head, size and position, layer, decorations and state.
It is read the way Fluxbox reads its own
.I ~/.fluxbox/apps
(Fluxbox's "Remember" feature), so a Fluxbox apps file works unchanged.
.PP
The file is
.I ~/.config/fluxland/apps
(or
.I ~/.fluxbox/apps
when fluxland uses a Fluxbox configuration directory); the init file's
.B session.appsFile
names another.
Like Fluxbox, fluxland reads the file again when it has changed and a
window opens, so edits apply to the next window without a reconfigure.
.SH SYNTAX
Blank lines and lines beginning with
.B #
or
.B !
are ignored.
.SS Entries
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
[app] (\fIpattern\fP) [(\fIpattern\fP) ...] [{\fIN\fP}]
  [\fISetting\fP] {\fIvalue\fP}
  ...
[end]
.fi
.RE
.PP
An
.B [app]
entry applies to the windows its client pattern matches (see
.BR "CLIENT PATTERNS" ).
Entries are tried from the top; a window takes the first that matches
it and keeps it until it closes.
.B [app]
entries match only main windows;
.B [transient]
entries, written the same way, match only dialogs (windows with a
parent).
.PP
.BI { N }
after the pattern limits the entry to
.I N
open windows at a time; later windows go on to the next entries.
.SS Groups
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
[group] [(\fIpattern\fP)]
  [app] (\fIpattern\fP)
  [app] (\fIpattern\fP)
  ...
  [\fISetting\fP] {\fIvalue\fP}
  ...
[end]
.fi
.RE
.PP
A window matching any of the group's
.B [app]
lines opens as a tab of a window of the same group that is already open.
The settings after the
.B [app]
lines apply to every window of the group.
The optional pattern on the
.B [group]
line picks which open window to join: with
.BR (workspace) ,
only one on the current workspace.
.SS Startup commands
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
[startup] [(screen=\fIN\fP)] {\fIcommand\fP}
.fi
.RE
.PP
Runs
.I command
when fluxland starts.
.SH CLIENT PATTERNS
A pattern is one or more terms in parentheses, all of which must match.
Each term is
.BI ( property = regex )
or
.BI ( property != regex ),
where
.I regex
is a POSIX extended regular expression that must match the whole value
(it is anchored, as in Fluxbox).
A term with no property, such as
.BR (xterm) ,
matches the name.
.TP
.B name
An X11 window's WM_CLASS instance; for a Wayland window, its app_id.
.TP
.B class
An X11 window's WM_CLASS class; for a Wayland window, its app_id.
.TP
.B title
The window title.
.TP
.B role
An X11 window's WM_WINDOW_ROLE.
.TP
.B tag
The tag a Wayland application gives a window (xdg-toplevel-tag), such as
.IR main-window ;
a fluxland extension.
.TP
.BR transient ", " maximized ", " minimized ", " fullscreen ", " shaded ", " stuck ", " urgent
.B yes
or
.BR no .
.TP
.BR workspace ", " workspacename ", " head ", " layer
Where the window is.
.B (workspace=[current])
(or just
.BR (workspace) )
matches windows on the current workspace.
.PP
Patterns are described in full in
.BR fluxland-keys (5).
.SH SETTINGS
Settings have the form
.BI [ Name "] {" value },
some with an option in parentheses.
Values are not case sensitive.
.TP
.BI [Workspace] " {" N }
Open on workspace
.I N
(counting from 0).
.TP
.BI [Jump] " {" yes | no }
With
.BR [Workspace] ,
also switch to that workspace.
.TP
.BI [Head] " {" N }
Open on head (monitor)
.IR N ,
counting from 1.
Dimensions and positions are then on that head.
.TP
.BI [Dimensions] " {" "W H" }
The window's size, decorations included.
Each value is in pixels, or a percentage of the head's usable area
(without the toolbar, slit and panels), as in
.BR "{50% 100%}" .
.TP
.BI [Position] " [(" corner ")] {" "X Y" }
Where the window opens: offsets in pixels or percent from a
.I corner
of the head's usable area.
The corner is
.B TopLeft
(the default; also
.BR UpperLeft ),
.B Top
(also
.BR TopCenter ),
.BR TopRight ,
.BR Left ,
.B Center
(also
.BR WinCenter ),
.BR Right ,
.BR BottomLeft ,
.BR Bottom ,
or
.BR BottomRight ,
and the Fluxbox spellings
.BR LeftTop ,
.BR Upper ,
.BR LowerLeft ,
.BR Lower ,
.BR LowerRight
and so on.
Offsets from a right or bottom corner count inwards:
.B (BottomRight) {10 10}
leaves 10 pixels to the edges.
.TP
.BI [Layer] " {" layer }
A Fluxbox layer number or name:
.B 0
.BR Menu ,
.B 2
.BR AboveDock ,
.B 4
.BR Dock ,
.B 6
.BR Top ,
.B 8
.BR Normal ,
.B 10
.BR Bottom ,
.B 12
.BR Desktop .
fluxland has four window layers: numbers below 8 (and
.BR Above )
are its top layer,
.B 10
(and
.BR Below )
its bottom one.
.TP
.BI [Deco] " {" decoration }
.B NORMAL
(full decorations),
.B NONE
(none),
.B BORDER
(border only),
.B TAB
(titlebar with tabs, no handle),
.B TINY
(titlebar with fewer buttons),
.B TOOL
(titlebar only), or a Fluxbox decoration bitmask such as
.BR 0x5 .
.TP
.BI [Alpha] " {" "focused [unfocused]" }
Opacity, 0\-255; one value is used for both.
.TP
.BI [Sticky] " {" yes | no }
On all workspaces.
.TP
.BI [Shaded] " {" yes | no }
Rolled up to its titlebar.
.TP
.BI [Minimized] " {" yes | no }
Open iconified.
.TP
.BI [Maximized] " {" yes | horz | vert | no }
Open maximized, or maximized in one direction.
.B no
refuses the application's own request to open maximized.
.TP
.BI [Fullscreen] " {" yes | no }
Open fullscreen;
.B no
refuses the application's request.
.TP
.BI [FocusHidden] " {" yes | no }
Leave the window out of window cycling (NextWindow).
.TP
.BI [IconHidden] " {" yes | no }
Leave the window out of the iconbar.
.TP
.BI [Hidden] " {" yes | no }
Both of the above.
.TP
.BI [FocusNewWindow] " {" yes | no }
Whether the window takes the focus when it opens, overriding
.BR session.screen0.focusNewWindows .
.TP
.BI [FocusProtection] " {" "flags" }
A comma-separated list:
.B Gain
(take the focus when opening),
.B Refuse
(don't),
.B Deny
(never take the focus by itself, for example when an application
asks to be activated),
.B Lock
(while this window has the focus, no other window takes it by itself),
or
.BR None .
.TP
.BI [Close] " {" yes | no }
When the window closes, save its state back into its entry.
.TP
.BI [IgnoreSizeHints] " {" yes | no }
Ignore the size the program asks to keep to: its minimum and maximum,
and the steps it grows in (a terminal's character cells, an X
program's aspect ratio).
.TP
.BI [Dock] " {" yes | no }
Put the window into the slit (fluxland extension).
.TP
.B [Tab]
Accepted for compatibility; like Fluxbox, it has no effect.
.SH REMEMBER
The window menu's
.B Remember...
submenu turns each setting of the window's entry on (as the window is
now) or off, one at a time; the
.B Remember
command writes all of the window's current state as its entry,
replacing the window's previous entry and keeping the rest of the file.
.PP
For a second after an X program's window opens, the program can't move
it from a remembered position or resize it from remembered dimensions
(or either, when it is remembered maximized or fullscreen).
.SH EXAMPLES
Open Firefox on workspace 2 and go there:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
[app] (class=firefox)
  [Workspace] {1}
  [Jump] {yes}
[end]
.fi
.RE
.PP
A terminal across the top half of the second monitor:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
[app] (name=xterm)
  [Head] {2}
  [Dimensions] {100% 50%}
  [Position] (Top) {0 0}
[end]
.fi
.RE
.PP
Center every dialog:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
[transient] (class=.*)
  [Position] (Center) {0 0}
[end]
.fi
.RE
.PP
Only the first mpv window, borderless and fullscreen:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
[app] (class=mpv) {1}
  [Deco] {NONE}
  [Fullscreen] {yes}
[end]
.fi
.RE
.PP
Terminals open as tabs of a terminal on the current workspace, below
other windows:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
[group] (workspace)
  [app] (class=foot)
  [app] (class=Alacritty)
  [Layer] {Bottom}
[end]
.fi
.RE
.SH COMPATIBILITY
fluxland 1.x read this file differently.
Patterns now match whole values: write
.B (class=.*term.*)
for "contains term".
.B [app]
entries no longer match dialogs; use
.BR [transient] .
.B [Layer]
numbers are Fluxbox's (8 is Normal), and
.B [Dimensions]
include the decorations.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR fluxland (1),
.BR fluxland-init (5),
.BR fluxland-keys (5),
.BR fluxland-menu (5),
.BR regex (7)
